#{[17592186425724 #edb/ref 1080 "II and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425724 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425724 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c2fcb20-55c1-502d-87bf-a84af1affda6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425725 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425725 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425725 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6dd9382b-2ca0-59aa-a584-c0b212c9c30e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425726 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425726 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425726 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f28cf69-6f55-526e-9f18-ac9d9a874611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425727 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "32b315b1-b85d-5621-9821-b0256f44b55f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-157376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425728 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(-1,3,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ea10c69-304c-5ad4-bd04-4affc2cf325b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425728 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(-a)+(-b) \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(-a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
